# Line endings are normalised: every text file is stored with LF in the
# repository and checked out with LF on every platform.
#
# Before this the tree carried both — 456 CRLF and 945 LF .py files, plus 6
# with BOTH endings inside one file.  Endings tracked whoever last wrote each
# file, so an editor that rewrote a whole file turned a two-line change into a
# whole-file diff and review had to be done with --ignore-cr-at-eol.  It was
# also converging on LF by accident, one branch at a time (LF +114 in a single
# week) — the same change, arriving without the review or the single commit.
#
# eol=lf rather than eol=native on purpose: native reintroduces the split the
# moment a Windows checkout appears, and this repo is developed on Linux and
# WSL2 where LF is already what every tool produces.
#
# NOTE for existing clones.  After pulling this, `git status` stays clean even
# where files on disk still hold CRLF: the text=auto clean filter normalises on
# read, so a CRLF working file and an LF blob compare equal.  The repository is
# normalised either way.  To make an existing working tree match, run:
#
#     git rm --cached -r . && git reset --hard
#
# NOTE for branches created before this commit.  Set
#
#     git config merge.renormalize true
#
# which normalises both sides before merging, so a pre-normalisation branch
# merges without a conflict on every line of every file it touches.
#
# See jaato #794.
* text=auto eol=lf
